    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d654">

                <pb facs="0810" n="524" sameAs="frus:frus1916Supp/pg524"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Great
                        Britain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Page</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1916-02-08">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 8, 1916</hi>
                        </date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2838. Your 3658, January 28. British Embassy informs Foreign Trade Adviser that
                    on January 18 nearly 150 eases, dating back to beginning of November, were in
                    Foreign Office still awaiting decision, notably Marshall Field and Company
                    submitted November 2 and November 18; that at present time not less than 150
                    cases are awaiting decision. Applications are not forwarded until details meet,
                    requirements of British Embassy. Therefore delay not due to Foreign Trade
                    Adviser. Urge strongly immediate decision on pending applications, especially
                    those held for two months or more. Importers indignant that repeated requests
                    for decisions are fruitless.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Lansing</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
